<span id="motivation"></span>
===Motivation===

A large body of deployed protocols share the same pattern: a sender performs a one-shot ECDH to a recipient's long-term public <code>secp256k1</code> key, derives a secret from it, and publishes something that depends on that secret on-chain.

As CRQC breaks ECDH with Shor's algorithm, every such protocol is exposed to a '''harvest-now-decrypt-later''' attack: an adversary records the published material today and, once a CRQC exists, recovers the shared secret and everything derived from it.
The exposure is retroactive and irreversible.
No key rotation, protocol upgrade, or later migration repairs data that has already been published.
Hence, the confidentiality of such protocols is at permanent risk and the post-quantum upgrade is needed as soon as possible.
One proposed remedy is a ''hybrid key encapsulation'': a secret is combined from two parts:

* classical ECDH key exchange
* post-quantum key encapsulation mechanism (PQ KEM, e.g. ML-KEM)

If one of the two parts is compromised, the scheme remains secure, e.g. if advances in cryptanalysis of lattice-based constructions compromise ML-KEM, classical security will still be guaranteed by ECDH.
For this reason, messaging protocols facing the same exposure are actively moving to hybrid key encapsulation (e.g. Signal's [https://signal.org/docs/specifications/pqxdh/ PQXDH] and Apple's [https://security.apple.com/blog/imessage-pq3/ iMessage PQ3]).
Another example is Bitcoin's encrypted peer-to-peer transport protocol [https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0324.mediawiki BIP324], which uses <code>secp256k1</code> ECDH.
Developers are actively discussing the hybrid replacement on the [https://groups.google.com/g/bitcoindev/c/n_5WuKVYqwI bitcoindev mailing list].

One possible important application of HKE is the [https://blockstream.com/bitcoin17-final41.pdf Confidential Assets] protocol, currently deployed on the Liquid network: sender and recipient establish a shared secret via ECDH and use it to derive a seed for a witness-recoverable range proof, letting the recipient later recover the amount and blinders under that same secret.
Because every range proof is published on-chain, an attacker equipped with a CRQC can retroactively break the ''confidentiality'' of every such output ever created.

==Description==

HKE is an instance of '''UG''', the framework that the CFRG draft [https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-irtf-cfrg-hybrid-kems/12/ ''Hybrid PQ/T Key Encapsulation Mechanisms''] (revision 12, §5.3) defines for combining a post-quantum KEM with a Diffie–Hellman group.
HKE instantiates it with <code>ML-KEM-768</code>, <code>secp256k1</code> and tagged SHA-256 (see [[#rationale|Rationale]]).

UG's combiner hashes both shared secrets, both ciphertexts and both public keys.
It is the combiner of [https://eprint.iacr.org/2018/024 Giacon, Heuer and Poettering] with the public keys added, and it has the shape that [https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/227/final NIST SP 800-227] (§4.6.3) recommends for combining KEMs.
[https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/039 X-Wing], which pairs <code>X25519</code> with <code>ML-KEM-768</code>, uses the combiner of the draft's CG framework instead: it omits the <code>ML-KEM</code> ciphertext and encapsulation key, and in exchange relies on a further security property of <code>ML-KEM-768</code> (see [[#security|Security]]).
HKE instead hashes them, which adds about 2.3 KB of SHA-256 input per encapsulation, but removes that dependency.

Both parts of the recipient's key are derived from one 32-byte secret <code>msk</code> with separately tagged hashes, so recovering the classical scalar from <code>B</code> with a CRQC does not reveal the ML-KEM decapsulation key.

==Specification==

===Notation===

* The group is <code>secp256k1</code> ([https://www.secg.org/sec2-v2.pdf SEC2], §2.4.1), prime order <code>n</code>, generator <code>G</code>. <code>k*P</code> is scalar multiplication; <code>O</code> is the point at infinity.
* <code>cbytes(P)</code>: the 33-byte compressed SEC1 encoding of a point <code>P</code> ([https://www.secg.org/sec1-v2.pdf SEC1], §2.3.3).
* <code>int(x)</code>: the integer with big-endian encoding <code>x</code>.
* <code>lift(x)</code>: the point <code>P</code> with <code>cbytes(P) = x</code>. It fails unless <code>|x| = 33</code>, <code>x[0]</code> is <code>0x02</code> or <code>0x03</code>, <code>X = int(x[1:33]) < p</code> (the field size), and <code>X</code> is the x coordinate of a point on the curve. <code>X</code> MUST NOT be reduced modulo <code>p</code>. If these hold, <code>P</code> is the point with x coordinate <code>X</code> whose <code>Y</code> is even for <code>0x02</code> and odd for <code>0x03</code>. <code>lift</code> never returns <code>O</code>, and <code>cbytes(lift(x)) = x</code> whenever <code>lift</code> succeeds.
* '''Tagged hash''', as defined in [https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0340.mediawiki BIP340]. For an ASCII tag <code>t</code>, <code>H_t(x) = SHA256(SHA256(t) || SHA256(t) || x)</code>.
* <code>||</code> is concatenation of byte strings.
* <code>len(x)</code> or <code>|x|</code>: the bytelength of serialized object <code>x</code>.
* <code>x $= S</code>: draw uniformly random <code>x</code> from the set <code>S</code>
* <code>K</code>: this document's 32-byte combiner output, the secret HKE ultimately delivers to an application. <code>K_c</code>, <code>K_q</code>: the classical (ECDH) and post-quantum (KEM) shared secrets combined into <code>K</code>.
* <code>label</code>: the 32-byte ASCII string <code>ELIP-HKE/v1/ML-KEM-768/secp256k1</code>, which ends the input to <code>H_seed</code> (as UG's <code>Label</code>).

This document uses the following tagged hash functions:

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Function
! Tag <code>t</code>
! Use
|-
| <code>H_blind</code>
| <code>ELIP-HKE/v1/blind</code>
| classical blinding scalar
|-
| <code>H_kem-d</code>
| <code>ELIP-HKE/v1/kem-d</code>
| KEM key-generation seed <code>d</code>
|-
| <code>H_kem-z</code>
| <code>ELIP-HKE/v1/kem-z</code>
| KEM key-generation seed <code>z</code>
|-
| <code>H_ecdh</code>
| <code>ELIP-HKE/v1/ecdh</code>
| classical shared secret
|-
| <code>H_seed</code>
| <code>ELIP-HKE/v1/seed/ML-KEM-768</code>
| combiner output; the tag names the parameter set
|}

In the terms of [https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-irtf-cfrg-hybrid-kems-12#section-5.3 UG], the seed is <code>msk</code>, <code>PRG(msk) = H_kem-d(msk) || H_kem-z(msk) || H_blind(msk)</code>, <code>RandomScalar(s) = int(s) mod n</code>, <code>ElementToSharedSecret(P) = H_ecdh(cbytes(P))</code>, <code>KDF = H_seed</code> and <code>Label = label</code>.

===KEM interface===

HKE uses <code>ML-KEM-768</code>, the category-3 parameter set of [https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/203/final FIPS 203], through the functions below, written with the prefix <code>KEM</code>.
Names follow FIPS 203, except that its shared secret key <code>K</code> is called <code>K_q</code> here, because <code>K</code> is the output of HKE.

* <code>KEM.KeyGen_internal(d, z) -> (ek, dk)</code> is <code>ML-KEM.KeyGen_internal</code> (FIPS 203, Algorithm 16). It takes two 32-byte seeds, <code>d</code> for the key pair and the implicit-rejection value <code>z</code>, and returns the encapsulation key <code>ek</code> of <code>L_ek = 1184</code> bytes and the decapsulation key <code>dk</code> of 2400 bytes.
* <code>KEM.Encaps_internal(ek, m) -> (K_q, c)</code> is <code>ML-KEM.Encaps_internal</code> (Algorithm 17). It takes <code>ek</code> and 32 bytes of randomness <code>m</code>, and returns the 32-byte shared secret key <code>K_q</code> and the ciphertext <code>c</code> of <code>L_c = 1088</code> bytes.
* <code>KEM.Decaps(dk, c) -> K_q</code> is <code>ML-KEM.Decaps</code> (Algorithm 21). FIPS 203 requires its inputs to be checked (§7.3): [[#decapsulation|Decapsulation]] checks <code>|c| = L_c</code>, and <code>dk</code> passes the decapsulation key checks because <code>KEM.KeyGen_internal</code> computed it.
* <code>KEM.CheckEK(ek) -> bool</code> is the encapsulation key check of FIPS 203 (§7.2), which has no function name there. It returns true iff <code>|ek| = L_ek</code> (type check) and every 12-bit coefficient decoded from the first <code>L_ek - 32</code> bytes of <code>ek</code> is less than <code>q = 3329</code> (modulus check).

===Key generation===

<pre>expandDecapsulationKey(msk):
    b := int(H_blind(msk)) mod n; fail if b = 0
    B := b*G
    (ek, dk) := KEM.KeyGen_internal(H_kem-d(msk), H_kem-z(msk))
    Return (b, dk, B, ek)

KeyGen():
    msk $= {0,1}^256
    (_, _, B, ek) := expandDecapsulationKey(msk)
    Return (msk, (cbytes(B), ek))</pre>

A recipient first calls <code>KeyGen</code> to generate a key pair:

* <code>msk</code>: the secret decapsulation key, which must be stored securely.
* <code>(B_bytes, ek)</code>: the public key, which the recipient publishes (e.g. via their address); <code>B_bytes = cbytes(B)</code> is 33 bytes and <code>ek</code> is <code>L_ek</code> bytes.

<code>KeyGen</code> draws <code>msk</code> uniformly at random to keep the description self-contained.
In practice, wallets derive <code>msk</code> deterministically under the key derivation rules of the application, so that a wallet restored from its backup recomputes <code>msk</code> and recovers its outputs.
This document does not fix that derivation, but the derivation MUST be a pseudorandom function, such as HMAC-SHA256, keyed with a secret that is used for nothing else and that an adversary with a CRQC cannot compute, and <code>msk</code> MUST NOT be used outside HKE; the [[#security|Security]] argument then holds unchanged.
For example, a Liquid wallet with the master blinding key <code>mbk</code> of [https://github.com/satoshilabs/slips/blob/master/slip-0077.md SLIP-77] can set <code>msk := HMAC-SHA256(H_t(mbk), scriptPubKey)</code> for a tag <code>t</code> of its own.
Keying HMAC with <code>mbk</code> itself would not do: that gives the SLIP-77 blinding key of the scriptPubKey, whose public key appears in the wallet's confidential addresses.

===Encapsulation===

Input: recipient public key <code>(B_bytes, ek)</code>, as byte strings.
Output: encryption key <code>K</code> and ciphertext <code>(E_bytes, c)</code>.

<pre>Encaps(B_bytes, ek):
    e $= [1, n-1]
    m $= {0,1}^256
    Return EncapsDerand(B_bytes, ek, e, m).

EncapsDerand(B_bytes, ek, e, m):
    B := lift(B_bytes); fail if lift fails.
    Fail if not KEM.CheckEK(ek).
    Fail if not 1 <= e <= n-1.
    Fail if |m| != 32.
    E := e*G
    E_bytes := cbytes(E)
    K_c := H_ecdh(cbytes(e*B))
    (K_q, c) := KEM.Encaps_internal(ek, m)
    K := H_seed(K_q || K_c || c || E_bytes || ek || B_bytes || label)
    Return (K, (E_bytes, c)).</pre>

<code>lift</code> rejects <code>O</code>, any encoding other than the 33-byte compressed one, and <code>X >= p</code>, so every public key has exactly one accepted encoding.
<code>Decaps</code> applies the same <code>lift</code> to <code>E_bytes</code>, so it hashes exactly the bytes that <code>Encaps</code> produced and no other encoding of <code>E</code> yields the same <code>K</code>.

<code>EncapsDerand</code> is the deterministic core of <code>Encaps</code> and exists for test vectors.
Implementations MUST use <code>Encaps</code>, drawing <code>e</code> and <code>m</code> fresh from a cryptographically secure source for every call.

A sender publishes <code>E_bytes</code> and <code>c</code> for the recipient (e.g. somewhere on-chain) and uses <code>K</code> to encrypt data to the recipient (e.g. in Liquid it seeds the DRBG for witness recoverable range proofs).

<span id="decapsulation"></span>
===Decapsulation===

Input: ciphertext <code>(E_bytes, c)</code> and the 32-byte decapsulation key <code>msk</code>.
Output: decryption key <code>K</code>.

<pre>Decaps((E_bytes, c), msk):
    (b, dk, B, ek) := expandDecapsulationKey(msk)
    E := lift(E_bytes); fail if lift fails.
    Fail if |c| != L_c.
    K_c := H_ecdh(cbytes(b*E))
    K_q := KEM.Decaps(dk, c)
    K := H_seed(K_q || K_c || c || cbytes(E) || ek || cbytes(B) || label)
    Return K.</pre>

Apart from the two format checks above, <code>Decaps</code> does not fail, except if <code>b = 0</code> in <code>expandDecapsulationKey</code>, which happens with probability <code>2^-255</code>.
<code>KEM.Decaps</code> uses implicit rejection: if <code>c</code> was not produced for <code>ek</code>, it returns an unrelated pseudorandom <code>K_q</code>, so the resulting <code>K</code> is simply wrong.
The recipient finds out only when the application tries to use <code>K</code> — in [[#confidential-assets|Confidential Assets]], the range-proof rewind fails.
Applications MUST treat this as &quot;output not recovered&quot;, not as a protocol error.

<span id="security"></span>
==Security==

HKE is an instance of UG, so the IND-CCA analyses that the CFRG draft gives for UG ([https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-irtf-cfrg-hybrid-kems-12#section-6.4.1 §6.4.1], detailed in [https://doi.org/10.62056/ahmp-49p1 Connolly and Grubbs]) apply to it.
Informally, with the tagged hashes modeled as random oracles, <code>K</code> is indistinguishable from random if either of the following holds:

# '''<code>ML-KEM-768</code> is IND-CCA secure.''' This covers quantum adversaries, with quantum access to the random oracles.
# '''The strong Diffie–Hellman problem on <code>secp256k1</code> is hard.''' This protects <code>K</code> against classical adversaries if <code>ML-KEM-768</code> is broken: computing <code>K_c</code> requires <code>e*B</code> from <code>B</code> and <code>E</code>, and, as in X-Wing, the proof answers decapsulation queries with a strong Diffie–Hellman oracle.

Condition 1 is the same for X-Wing, whose post-quantum proof also needs only IND-CCA security ([https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/039 X-Wing], Theorem 2).
Condition 2 is where hashing <code>c</code> matters.
X-Wing does not hash <code>c</code>, so its classical proof (Theorem 1) also needs <code>ML-KEM-768</code> to be ciphertext second-preimage resistant (C2PRI).
Without that property, an adversary could find a ciphertext <code>c' != c*</code> that decapsulates to the same <code>K_q</code> as the challenge <code>(E*, c*)</code>, and query the decapsulation of <code>(E*, c')</code> to obtain the challenge key.
In HKE, <code>c'</code> changes the input to <code>H_seed</code>, which the proof models as a random oracle, so that query yields an independent <code>K</code>, and condition 2 needs nothing from <code>ML-KEM-768</code>.

Hashing both ciphertexts and both public keys also makes <code>K</code> binding, assuming only that <code>H_seed</code> is collision resistant.
Even an adversary that knows the decapsulation keys cannot make two different ciphertexts, or ciphertexts for two different public keys, decapsulate to the same <code>K</code> (LEAK-BIND-K-CT and LEAK-BIND-K-PK, [https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-irtf-cfrg-hybrid-kems-12#section-6.4.2.1 CFRG draft, §6.4.2.1]).

HKE does not provide forward secrecy (compromise of <code>msk</code> exposes every <code>K</code> sent to it), sender authentication, deniability, or an explicit failure signal (see [[#decapsulation|Decapsulation]]).

<span id="rationale"></span>
==Rationale==

HKE is designed for Bitcoin-based chains, such as Bitcoin and Liquid, and for protocols that use the same primitives, such as Nostr.
These use <code>secp256k1</code> for keys and SHA-256 for hashing, so HKE keeps both and adds only what they lack, a post-quantum KEM.

===secp256k1 and SHA-256===

With <code>secp256k1</code>, the classical part of HKE is the ECDH these protocols already perform, with the same point encodings and the same implementations, such as [https://github.com/bitcoin-core/secp256k1 <code>libsecp256k1</code>] and [https://github.com/BlockstreamResearch/secp256k1-zkp <code>secp256k1-zkp</code>].
A wallet that adopts HKE needs no second curve.
Every hash in HKE is a tagged hash, the domain-separated SHA-256 that BIP340 defines, [https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0341.mediawiki BIP341] and [https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0352.mediawiki BIP352] use, and <code>libsecp256k1</code> implements.
<code>H_seed</code> serves as UG's <code>KDF</code>: its tag separates it from the other hashes, as for every tagged hash here, and <code>label</code> ends its input because UG's combiner ends with one.

===ML-KEM-768===

The encapsulation key <code>ek</code> is part of the recipient's public key, and a ciphertext <code>c</code> is published with every encapsulation, so their sizes are the main criterion for choosing the KEM.
<code>ML-KEM</code> ([https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/203/final FIPS 203]) has smaller keys and ciphertexts than [https://pqc-hqc.org/doc/hqc_specifications_2025_08_22.pdf HQC], the only other KEM [https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/ir/8545/final NIST has selected], which at category 3 has a 4514-byte <code>ek</code> and an 8978-byte <code>c</code>.
[https://eprint.iacr.org/2018/383 CSIDH], the main alternative outside NIST's selection, needs public values of about 1 KB at category 3 ([https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/1520 SQALE]), close to the 1184-byte <code>ek</code> and 1088-byte <code>c</code> of <code>ML-KEM-768</code>, and its group action is orders of magnitude slower.

HKE uses <code>ML-KEM-768</code> (category 3), which FIPS 203 (§8) recommends as the default parameter set because it &quot;provides a large security margin at a reasonable performance cost&quot;.
X-Wing, which targets category 1, also uses <code>ML-KEM-768</code> rather than <code>ML-KEM-512</code>, as a hedge against future cryptanalysis ([https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-connolly-cfrg-xwing-kem/ X-Wing draft]).
The margin matters for HKE because a ciphertext, once published, stays exposed to any later attack ([[#motivation|Motivation]]).
<code>ML-KEM-1024</code> (category 5) would add 384 bytes to <code>ek</code> and 480 bytes to every <code>c</code>.

==Applications==

HKE fits a protocol when the following conditions hold.

# '''The protocol already uses <code>secp256k1</code> for its classical key exchange.''' If not, use X-Wing directly; HKE exists to reuse the curve and key infrastructure that is already deployed, not to introduce a second curve.
# '''The exchange is one-shot and non-interactive.''' If the parties can complete a round trip, use a hybrid handshake with forward secrecy instead.
# '''The adversary can store the published data until a CRQC exists.''' Harvest-now-decrypt-later only works if the attacker keeps the transcript, so HKE helps more the more public and long-lived that data is. On-chain data is public and permanent, making HKE the ideal choice for replacing the original protocol.
# '''There is somewhere to put ~1 KB, and paying for it is affordable at the protocol's natural frequency.''' A KEM ciphertext has no second use in the protocol that carries it, so it is pure overhead. Whether that overhead is acceptable depends far more on how ''often'' it is paid than on its size.

<span id="confidential-assets"></span>
===Confidential Assets===

Every confidential output on Liquid ([https://blockstream.com/bitcoin17-final41.pdf Confidential Assets]) carries an ephemeral public key <code>E</code> and derives its range-proof seed from <code>ECDH(e, B)</code>, where <code>B</code> is the recipient's long-term blinding key.
Both <code>E</code> and <code>B</code> are public, so a CRQC recovers the seed of any recorded output and, by rewinding its range proof, the amount, the asset and both blinders: the protocol is exposed to ''harvest-now-decrypt-later''.

Replacing <code>ECDH(e, B)</code> with HKE and using its shared secret <code>K</code> as the range-proof seed requires the recipient's address to carry the ML-KEM encapsulation key <code>ek</code> alongside <code>B</code>, and each confidential output to carry the ciphertext <code>c</code> alongside <code>E</code>.
The range-proof code is unchanged, since it takes a 32-byte seed either way.

===BIP47 reusable payment codes===

In [https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0047.mediawiki BIP47], Alice sends Bob her payment code once, in a ''notification transaction'', and both then derive the address of each payment from an ECDH between keys of their two payment codes.
Payment codes are meant to be published, so a CRQC that knows both codes derives every address of the relationship, whether or not the notification is protected.

HKE fits a new payment-code version in which Bob's payment code carries <code>ek</code> (about 1.3 KB instead of 80 bytes), the notification carries <code>c</code>, and each payment's secret is derived from <code>K</code> and a counter rather than from the payment codes.
One ciphertext per counterparty then protects every payment of the relationship.
Alice must store <code>K</code>, since she cannot recompute it from her seed, and an observer still sees that Alice notified Bob.

===Nostr===

In [https://github.com/nostr-protocol/nips/blob/master/44.md NIP-44], encrypted direct messages derive a conversation key from a static-static ECDH on <code>secp256k1</code> and publish the ciphertext to relays, which retain it indefinitely.
The specification already records the gap: ''&quot;No post-quantum security: a powerful quantum computer would be able to decrypt the messages.&quot;''
There is no carrier problem, since an event has no practical size limit, which makes this the least constrained application discussed here.
One adaptation is needed: NIP-44's conversation key is symmetric, satisfying <code>conv(a, B) == conv(b, A)</code>, and a KEM breaks that symmetry because only the recipient holds <code>dk</code>.
Either the sender retains <code>K_q</code>, or each party encapsulates to the other.

===Encryption to a known public key===

Wallet backups, PSET blobs, and output descriptors are routinely sealed to a <code>secp256k1</code> key the sender already holds.
No protocol constrains the size or the frequency, so HKE applies directly and needs nothing further specified here.

==Backwards Compatibility==

HKE is a new primitive and changes no existing protocol.

==Test Vectors and Reference Code==

A Python reference implementation of the Specification section is in [[elip-hybrid-key-encapsulation/hke.py|<code>elip-hybrid-key-encapsulation/hke.py</code>]], with one function per pseudocode definition.
It uses a vendored copy of the [https://github.com/secp256k1lab/secp256k1lab/ secp256k1lab] library at version 1.0.0 (commit [https://github.com/secp256k1lab/secp256k1lab/commit/44dc4bd893b8f03e621585e3bf255253e0e0fbfb 44dc4bd893b8f03e621585e3bf255253e0e0fbfb]), and [https://github.com/GiacomoPope/kyber-py <code>kyber-py</code>] 1.2.0 for ML-KEM-768, which passes NIST's ACVP test vectors for FIPS 203.
Test vectors are in [[elip-hybrid-key-encapsulation/vectors.json|<code>elip-hybrid-key-encapsulation/vectors.json</code>]] and are produced by [[elip-hybrid-key-encapsulation/gen_vectors.py|<code>elip-hybrid-key-encapsulation/gen_vectors.py</code>]] in the same directory.
Each vector lists its inputs under <code>given</code> and the values an implementation must compute under <code>expected</code>; failure vectors have no <code>expected</code>.
They cover:

* '''Valid:''' <code>msk</code>, <code>e</code> and <code>m</code> as inputs; <code>b</code>, <code>B</code>, <code>ek</code>, <code>dk</code>, <code>E_bytes</code>, <code>c</code>, <code>K_c</code>, <code>K_q</code> and <code>K</code> as outputs. They include points <code>B</code>, <code>E</code> and <code>e*B</code> whose x coordinate starts with a zero byte, which catch an x coordinate encoded in fewer than 32 bytes. They also include a <code>K_c</code> and a <code>K_q</code> that start with a zero byte, which catch an implementation that does not handle them as fixed 32-byte strings.
* '''Valid encapsulation:''' <code>B_bytes</code>, <code>ek</code>, <code>e</code> and <code>m</code> as inputs; <code>E_bytes</code>, <code>c</code>, <code>K_c</code>, <code>K_q</code> and <code>K</code> as outputs, for an <code>ek</code> that <code>KeyGen</code> does not produce but <code>KEM.CheckEK</code> accepts: each 3-byte group packs <code>q - 1</code> next to <code>q - 2</code>, so that a decoder that reads a bit from the wrong place sees a coefficient <code>>= q</code>, and the last 32 bytes are <code>0xff</code>.
* '''Implicit rejection:''' ciphertexts that decapsulate without error to a <code>K</code> different from the encapsulated one, with that <code>K</code> and its <code>K_c</code> and <code>K_q</code> given.
* '''Decapsulation failure:''' an invalid <code>E_bytes</code> or a wrong <code>|c|</code>. The invalid <code>E_bytes</code> include the 65-byte uncompressed and hybrid encodings, which a general SEC1 parser such as <code>secp256k1_ec_pubkey_parse</code> accepts, the 32-byte x-only encoding of BIP340, 33 zero bytes, which BIP327 decodes as the point at infinity, and <code>X >= p</code> where <code>X - p</code> is a valid x coordinate, which catches an implementation that reduces <code>X</code> modulo <code>p</code>.
* '''Encapsulation failure:''' an invalid <code>B_bytes</code> (the same classes as <code>E_bytes</code>), an <code>ek</code> that fails <code>KEM.CheckEK</code> (a wrong <code>|ek|</code>, including valid <code>ML-KEM-512</code> and <code>ML-KEM-1024</code> keys, or a coefficient <code>>= q</code> at the first and last position of each of the three polynomials, which covers both 12-bit halves of a 3-byte group), an <code>e</code> outside <code>[1, n-1]</code> (including <code>n + 1</code>, which catches an implementation that reduces <code>e</code> modulo <code>n</code>), or a wrong <code>|m|</code>.

No vector reaches the reduction modulo <code>n</code> or the <code>b = 0</code> failure in <code>expandDecapsulationKey</code>: each happens with probability at most about <code>2^-128</code>.

